 overall well-being of society. One of the reasons for limited youth participation in politics is the lack of awareness and education about political processes and systems. Many young people do not fully understand how politics affects their lives, and they may feel disconnected from the political sphere. This lack of knowledge can create a sense of apathy and disinterest, leading to low youth voter turnout and a lack of engagement in political activities. Another barrier to youth participation in politics is the perception that politics is a realm reserved for older individuals with more experience and knowledge. Young people may feel that their voices are not valued or that they do not have the necessary skills to participate in political discussions. This belief can be reinforced by the absence of young leaders in positions of power, further discouraging youth involvement. Furthermore, the influence of money and special interest groups in politics can also deter young individuals from participating. The high cost of political campaigns and the close ties between politicians and wealthy donors can create a perception that politics is a game for the elite. Young people without financial resources may feel discouraged and perceive their participation as futile. To encourage youth participation in politics, it is crucial to address these barriers and create an inclusive environment that values and supports young voices. Education programs that teach young people about political processes, voting rights, and the importance of civic engagement can help increase awareness and interest in politics. Additionally, mentorship programs that connect young individuals with experienced politicians and activists can provide guidance and support for those interested in pursuing a political career. Furthermore, it is essential to promote diversity and inclusivity in politics, ensuring that young people from all backgrounds have an equal opportunity to participate. Creating spaces 